---
title: Post-interceptors
description: Shaping, caching and auditing the result after a handler succeeds.
---

A post-interceptor runs after the main handler and returns the result the caller will
receive. Like the pre stage, the return value is the point: a post-interceptor can observe
the result, replace it, or derive a new one.

```csharp
public sealed class AuditCreation : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    private readonly IAuditLog _audit;

    public AuditCreation(IAuditLog audit) => _audit = audit;

    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(
        CreateProduct command, Guid result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _audit.Record("product.created", result);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Post-interceptors run **only on success**. If the handler threw, the post stage is skipped and
the [exception stage](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors) takes its
place; anything that must run either way belongs in a
[final interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/final-interceptors). After an
`Abort()` nothing downstream runs at all — not the post stage, and not the final stage.
{% /aside %}

## Shaping a result

The typed two-parameter form is the one to reach for when you are transforming:

```csharp
public sealed class MaskSecrets : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetUser, UserDto>
{
    public ValueTask<UserDto> HandleAsync(
        GetUser query, UserDto result, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(result with { Email = Mask(result.Email) });
}
```

Each post-interceptor in the stage sees the previous one's output, so transformations
compose in `[Weight]` order.

## Caching

Populate the cache after a successful query, and check it in the matching pre-interceptor:

```csharp
public sealed class CacheProduct : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IMemoryCache _cache;

    public CacheProduct(IMemoryCache cache) => _cache = cache;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, Product? result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (result is not null)
            _cache.Set(query.Id, result, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));

        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

The read side has no interceptor to live in: since v2.3 nothing before the handler can supply
the pipeline's result — `Abort` cuts the pipeline rather than completing it. Read the cache in
the handler, or in a handler that decorates the real one.

## Blanket auditing

The non-generic form applies to every command in the module. It receives the result as
`object`, since there is no single type it could be:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordOutcome : ICommandPostInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Increment($"command.{command.GetType().Name}.ok");
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Because this matches covariantly, a message can opt out with `[ExcludeFromPipeline]` — see
[Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/polymorphism#opting-out).

## Events

An event has no result, so the event post-interceptor receives the `ValueTask` representing
the handler invocation rather than a value:

```csharp
public sealed class LogPublished : IEventPostInterceptor<OrderPlaced>
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        OrderPlaced @event, ValueTask result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Published {Event}", @event.Id);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(result);
    }
}
```

There is no two-parameter typed form for event post-interceptors, for the same reason.

## Coverage

| Interface | `HandleAsync` |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `IQueryPostInterceptor` / `<TQuery>` / `<TQuery, TResult>` | as above |
| `IEventPostInterceptor` / `<TEvent>` | `ValueTask<object>(TEvent, ValueTask, ErgosfareContext)` |

Next: [Exception interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors).
